#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183862 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "0431c02c-9a72-5d5b-ad75-550f413b74f1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10025" :question/problem "The graph above shows the function $y = \\tan(Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/eb20bf9f9c01a9caff32aae31f5d3d3c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{5} - \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{5}\\right) = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function\n\n$$\ny = A\\tan(Bx + C) + D\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B} \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. The graph shows that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D = 1$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nB + D =\\dfrac{5}{2} +1 = \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny = \\tan\\left(\\frac{5x}{2} \\right) +1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183999 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "fdd0c8ab-6977-5a8f-b1db-37ae9b2b116e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3663" :question/problem "The graph below shows the function $y=\\cot{(Bx)}+D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What are the values of $B$ and $D$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/a5320a03808d49301adbe9c8f8dd0122.png)" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac \\pi 2\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function\n\n$$\ny=A\\cot(Bx + C) + D\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac \\pi 2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{B}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\left(\\dfrac \\pi 2\\right)} = 2\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit down. Therefore, $D=-1$.\n\nSo, the final equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\cot\\left(2x\\right)-1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184000 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "90986bdd-15dd-51c6-b5b0-4174542736c9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3669" :question/problem "The graph above shows the function $y=\\tan{\\left(2x+C\\right)}$, where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} < C < \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, what is the value of $C$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/9ca2e643947437ad9a25d7a152252f4b.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\tan (2x + C) \\\\\n&= \\tan (2(x + \\frac{C}{2}))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\tan 2x$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\n$$\n\nunits to the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{8}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad C = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250641 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9802eb8-3fbe-5dec-a5a2-e38dcba2d37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250643} {:answer/id #uuid "43c95071-cc23-5c59-85e9-6847e9350c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250644} {:answer/id #uuid "04bcb005-98cd-5cad-b7d8-009b96a173e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250645} {:answer/id #uuid "56204d13-e746-5592-8848-2dc92e919d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250646} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9f969d-cf1d-51f1-ab5a-72431daf59ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04bcb005-98cd-5cad-b7d8-009b96a173e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250645} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8782fa4-99e0-56ca-9e87-9316a4149688" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250642}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e52e5ebc-a1b0-593c-b883-50f2676ea209" :question/math-academy-id "q-117795" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/0f920d90aa215300e720ace10bfec9292440d20f32c9b907854b984014f77427.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d6103da-a933-5f3c-8237-e5d132840235" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250650} {:answer/id #uuid "92860296-dfb3-5191-91ee-b4a0c5141c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250651} {:answer/id #uuid "337dced6-8a5a-546e-b2e9-0a32904fde55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250652} {:answer/id #uuid "8b11421c-f405-508e-a3c5-f0e8804caa0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250653} {:answer/id #uuid "119f49a0-bc34-50b8-b276-600854b8e6b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250654}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92860296-dfb3-5191-91ee-b4a0c5141c29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250651} :answer-field/id #uuid "6606a1ed-6024-5de9-8007-316331c1b133" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250649}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ecbe368-922d-5a9f-956d-5a27886e10df" :question/math-academy-id "q-117796" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/9278723975d70fec73e313d0e164908ddd339231cb74f75b07c2edce14680111.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-(-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{1}{2}$ unit up. Hence, $D=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f8ae70b-92d9-5cc9-a5be-dd6531ddc6dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250657} {:answer/id #uuid "168b009b-eb74-530b-9138-85aec12ac989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250658} {:answer/id #uuid "6a2b64b3-85a3-5025-be6e-aafff2391545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250659} {:answer/id #uuid "96fa204e-098d-5cda-9798-e6c188251b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250660} {:answer/id #uuid "07bc4b07-a41a-563e-9c73-730b3c518709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07bc4b07-a41a-563e-9c73-730b3c518709" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250661} :answer-field/id #uuid "091351e4-7a45-5333-a5a0-b28f52c127a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250656}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c44f6521-15eb-5c8e-93a8-cab320c1122a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117800" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/6003a85e0038b7e6562381cd338a7597877d1152adbeb476b7141eee74ba06ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-0=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{5}{4}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{5}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2})+\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ee060b1-3746-5e0b-83ad-ec50cd9c83a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250664} {:answer/id #uuid "8e15aa35-000e-57ad-a13f-5431f1957930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250665} {:answer/id #uuid "1699d48c-fb09-5584-bf02-fb59e39c4f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250666} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e0fcc6-a4c0-5bd9-bce9-36959fcdcc35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250667} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4ba13e-21cc-539e-8498-077deeb957c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4ba13e-21cc-539e-8498-077deeb957c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250668} :answer-field/id #uuid "4792ce8e-51ae-5527-b281-d69c7574ea26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250663}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc965e10-4874-590e-8c18-9692661389da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141974" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/2b8bbc9ae6b8bf8c441c8e2b534fb159f8180833e1891baa094f988d6a362117.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $D-B?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4})=\\frac{6\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ units up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $D-B=1-(\\frac{2}{3})=\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1479efa3-d543-58b8-bb72-0b25587485c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250671} {:answer/id #uuid "87ada3ca-84dc-5706-9582-97a08b701b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250672} {:answer/id #uuid "140de80c-4f26-53f3-a381-356ae8ac989c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250673} {:answer/id #uuid "b9df9813-3fad-5150-a996-c3422f5ef7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250674} {:answer/id #uuid "bbdb9508-60e9-581c-850a-2530f82d7c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9df9813-3fad-5150-a996-c3422f5ef7a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250674} :answer-field/id #uuid "18c2557b-a4e9-54c8-b446-9bda2769a428" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250670}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "099318bb-f16c-592c-8396-8642f6dbb6d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-143638" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/112ee04f02087fbf9f477a4cc002a7b24f7881e1ac2e37a1566ccc43e948cf0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{4}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\pi }=2.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $2$ unit down. Hence, $D=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=2+(-2)=0.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cd468a0-9b78-530a-a9b3-4d5d92a61124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250678} {:answer/id #uuid "eec6d319-df77-5fee-a246-a68e8ccc6599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250679} {:answer/id #uuid "04b8ff0c-3594-5f2b-9085-89943bf5318e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250680} {:answer/id #uuid "f082efb1-4250-5c5e-b202-93cbc4bd2c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250681} {:answer/id #uuid "f6e73a94-f30c-5985-93e9-e5e8fece272d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{23}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f082efb1-4250-5c5e-b202-93cbc4bd2c1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250681} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d05ad20-f842-564d-9b39-68acf66fba8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250677}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea1ca9e7-5ad3-5b9e-a96e-f5ccff3aca09" :question/math-academy-id "q-143665" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/39d3bd73ac0f60f21cc9e71cbf46d62901fcc07cab3b06cd84a496b1e1415a1a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $5$ units down. Hence, $D=-5.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{2}+(-5)=-\\frac{7}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})-5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cf71456-e433-5f2e-a7e9-9c96c8a84c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250685} {:answer/id #uuid "a9f3b74f-4ebc-55f9-95d6-162cfe8df179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250686} {:answer/id #uuid "5a878c8d-02ea-5839-90c7-fc1ac2afaa26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250687} {:answer/id #uuid "d4951cd9-0945-56bd-a84a-946ff91b45e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250688} {:answer/id #uuid "6117de41-0aa9-518b-9753-471cb40e4c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cf71456-e433-5f2e-a7e9-9c96c8a84c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250685} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd574ae3-6c39-5abd-92e9-c19a6010730b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250684}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af1bf5b7-d6f3-5371-8c7d-a519f70d9653" :question/math-academy-id "q-143670" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/c2e5b01cf03f1f9d82562ee51fc484448e90b74dd2584daf18a499849ca36bff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+3C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}(x+3C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/29346a073d6a22f0bab74eeac86431e8c761c9ed21a005ec6965f71e6436c117.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})$ by $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n3C=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250690 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2469eb49-f945-51a6-bd67-385181ca9534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250692} {:answer/id #uuid "88175730-87a0-57dc-b329-d16999ac8f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250693} {:answer/id #uuid "b856d1ba-4cd2-57e0-9ec0-38c7ecf1ab64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250694} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c2adbd-5475-5287-90ba-9d5e3581e2dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250695} {:answer/id #uuid "a9b6ab24-a082-5ef0-9200-3272b8d65bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b856d1ba-4cd2-57e0-9ec0-38c7ecf1ab64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250694} :answer-field/id #uuid "7062cce4-e6fb-5055-884a-402f9e6d7825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed308f35-6936-5e15-9ba0-feff8b2097a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-143675" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/a8fbbaff1139a0c80c5dc97a9d99a08e238a61ab65251a158b382e208be7e842.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{4}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4(x+\\frac{C}{4}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/4c5147d95cc4d5df1d847f5fe9f580d9595d0f4d8c2c8209efa1f2a3cd9c5aea.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x)$ by $\\frac{9\\pi }{40}-\\frac{\\pi }{8}=\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ units to the right. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b673c6a-01e8-5d9e-8f0f-74d7c698a37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250699} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d5962b-c7d1-5864-858d-fe3f1d39738a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250700} {:answer/id #uuid "5babeeef-a8f1-511a-b58f-bed13852c167" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250701} {:answer/id #uuid "c32bdca9-cd10-5d08-bf2a-49619ae4741c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250702} {:answer/id #uuid "1111f967-7f83-575f-ac96-99557c1be78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1111f967-7f83-575f-ac96-99557c1be78b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250703} :answer-field/id #uuid "a13a5095-aa8b-5e41-9483-27f8aedf50a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250698}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3090bea4-a609-5a53-a6eb-a8060c8d0e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143677" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/7602d1695610361f28613d8bf3542056b2624f9b6b8980873f6fdebf802c6750.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{3C}{2}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}(x+\\frac{3C}{2}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/015ca9336646a7545de842414ace2c2a695688e30653b2ed019000d69609d56d.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})$ by $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3C}{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{2}{3})\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86772212-906f-5549-b0a8-da556aa99a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183504} {:answer/id #uuid "649c3f40-f63c-5873-b39a-b3141cdea0d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183505} {:answer/id #uuid "703a8949-5d98-5297-8715-deee3aef7e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183506} {:answer/id #uuid "70512bc7-97c8-5498-9f79-6bf61335104b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183507} {:answer/id #uuid "7d76318c-ff9b-541a-b4dd-2ecf529dd032" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "703a8949-5d98-5297-8715-deee3aef7e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183506} :answer-field/id #uuid "179fae05-4da2-53cd-9be1-6068cea66c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183503}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf5f1258-fe1b-5b76-b089-dbb4191effcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-65448" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/q-65448.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan (Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\pi -(-\\pi )=2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\pi }=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $1$ unit up. Hence, $D=1.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{1}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da46f0fb-47e3-503c-847d-4d35ab89921f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183522} {:answer/id #uuid "22a0e5f4-b7e7-5afb-9a24-70f89519e272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183523} {:answer/id #uuid "244eff46-7e8d-5062-ae2c-47a4fd830205" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183524} {:answer/id #uuid "57d59743-adf7-58df-ab27-3ecb712cc4c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183525} {:answer/id #uuid "69b01c25-9ce9-5c60-96ae-2eb25a3a9523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183526}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da46f0fb-47e3-503c-847d-4d35ab89921f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183522} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbca80b9-13c3-5bda-a289-8104e727c621" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183521}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91496801-bb35-528a-8bb8-dd007b8e0c83" :question/math-academy-id "q-65549" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/q-65549.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\cot (Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B - 2D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{3}{2}$ units up. Hence, $D=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B-2D=5-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})=5-3=2.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1c2c1eb-db74-5e2c-bd88-9f3da1804b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250706} {:answer/id #uuid "b49b83fd-1776-57d2-abe2-14ebdae03ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250707} {:answer/id #uuid "1f62e4b8-261e-5898-a374-81526ea71b04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250708} {:answer/id #uuid "31136edc-d135-5bc5-9b5d-0b937c221afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250709} {:answer/id #uuid "d3ccaef8-8575-5851-a29a-8efa68f8a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250710}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3ccaef8-8575-5851-a29a-8efa68f8a05e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250710} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d443759-daaa-55fa-bfa3-463af17a09d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250705}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a31794b-90ed-5858-8a23-bda34267af75" :question/math-academy-id "q-65550" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/c904a1eb628e726647a6f655a567a0939f7b7d404f0b32c6c4b4adaeca847916.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B-D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B-D=(\\frac{5}{4})-(-3)=\\frac{5}{4}+3=\\frac{17}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be98e567-8169-5794-839b-bbf503f04195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183528} {:answer/id #uuid "eca9dc9d-dbde-5bb5-a83e-bf433e729282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183529} {:answer/id #uuid "129f0f1a-adcd-53a3-95f9-71c4b9923bca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183530} {:answer/id #uuid "1d5645af-178f-5ffe-8f67-f5e3a5973990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183531} {:answer/id #uuid "4f56ba02-7c47-57dc-81fd-f2968dc04103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eca9dc9d-dbde-5bb5-a83e-bf433e729282" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183529} :answer-field/id #uuid "08203091-ceba-5827-8c95-608cb5e4e6ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183527}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "397bffc1-1dbf-51bd-b606-1b9fd6b7e36a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65570" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/q-65570.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\tan (\\frac{x}{2} + C)$, where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{π}{2} < C < \\frac{π}{2}$, what is the value of $C$?" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+2C$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x+2C))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=tan\\,(\\frac{x}{2}).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/8a19beac86998c845d7ffa225a52d425ab48b9ec350f6c8fe42f7c6dbce027ce.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n2C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aad4bfcf-ac83-5c47-928b-5b1a7fc0f104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183612} {:answer/id #uuid "df3e3991-8f50-5de8-b75b-5960efe17e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183613} {:answer/id #uuid "f2572169-df3d-5c99-975a-9f3f678ee3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183614} {:answer/id #uuid "f28c8896-b9fe-5342-9cba-cebeee74006f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183615} {:answer/id #uuid "095c622d-5db2-5a48-942e-b312abb3f7c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df3e3991-8f50-5de8-b75b-5960efe17e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183613} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d980f41-06e1-5562-995c-dd718d6a8c7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183611}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d92d6b12-34ec-5e74-a8fa-e75755bbf55a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79188" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3850/Source/Images/q-79188.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y = \\cot (Bx) + D$, where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B + D$?" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units up. Hence, $D=3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=3+3=6.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)+3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a342a72-b5db-52a8-bbbc-4f6c459093e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250713} {:answer/id #uuid "c2abc93f-c5ef-5767-9f98-f5421b4b7328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250714} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae513fa-fd63-570f-961b-db18f9c86ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250715} {:answer/id #uuid "a91c85b7-87bb-59ab-8ea8-5c95f155f09f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250716} {:answer/id #uuid "31d82c98-47ea-5443-b60d-d110accf3073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2abc93f-c5ef-5767-9f98-f5421b4b7328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250714} :answer-field/id #uuid "36837e76-b2da-51d2-a076-35871200dfc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adbc630c-edd8-52e4-9bd9-f612c8f44da5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79195" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/89efc20f1c6527d1bcd501f97ce585fb5a60e9791804f56d61f33653cf1a70d7.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943521/assets/f8617da98ea9d9fb48b8fef588b624c6961a299233836fe433c1819b697ca0fb.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{12})=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ units to the left. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"}]}
